  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places trough stretching from western\nSaskatchewan through eastern Montana/Wyoming, with high over the\nmidwest. Upper level analysis places high over New Mexico, with\nridge stretching northward to the lee of the Rockies into the\nPlains. Over our area, low level moisture continues to be on \nthe increase with south-southeasterly low level flow between the\ntwo aforementioned surface features. A line of showers with a \nfew embedded thunderstorms continues to slide through western \nNorth Dakota affiliated with a short wave trough noted in the\nflow.\n\nFor the rest of this afternoon into this evening, a tricky\nforecast is on tap as some variables remain in play. It has \nbeen well advertised that with the returning moisture we will \nhave an unstable atmosphere in place, particularly over \nsouthwest into south central North Dakota where CAPE values are\nalready in excess of 2000 J/Kg at this time over a broad part \nof the area, and that plenty of deep layer shear in in place \n(40-50 kts 0-6km) to support rotating updrafts. With that said, \nthe flies in the ointment continue to be rather modest forcing, \nalong with forecast soundings showing a cap that may be \ndifficult to overcome. Latest short term guidance continues to \nshow a variety of solutions ranging from some light showers/a \nfew weak storms to a variety of severe over the southwest and \nsouth central, possible nudging northward. So, the severe threat\nwill remain conditional, but if a storm or storms were able to \nget going in earnest, large hail and damaging wind gusts remain \na concern given the aforementioned shear/instability. A \nsecondary wave pushes in from Montana later tonight, which may \nlead to some additional elevated storms, which would move \nthrough overnight.\n\nOn Monday, surface trough to the west pushes over western North\nDakota in the morning, into central North Dakota before stalling\nmid-day. Behind the trough, drier air will move in with breezy\nconditions developing, which will lead to some near-critical\nfire weather conditions over the far west. Ahead of the trough,\nmoist air remains in place. CAPE values will again be elevated\nover this area, along with similar deep layer shear as today.\nAgain, though, the threat remains conditional as to whether\nenough forcing will be in place in order for storms to develop.\n\nOn Tuesday, quiet weather is expected as the ridge axis re-\nfocuses over the Rockies, though a compact low/short wave will \nzip through southern Saskatchewan/Manitoba which may lead to a \nfew showers or possibly a thunderstorm around the Turtle \nMountains region. Northwest winds will be on the increase as the\nsurface low with this system moves east through Manitoba into \nOntario. Upper ridge gradually meanders east resulting in quiet \nweather for us through Thursday before a few weak waves move \nthrough the ridge, bringing some low precipitation chances to \nend the week.",
